.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_IN_INIT 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_in_before ,
.Nm SSL_in_init ,
.Nm SSL_is_init_finished ,
.Nm SSL_in_connect_init ,
.Nm SSL_in_accept_init ,
.Nm SSL_get_state
.Nd retrieve information about the handshake state machine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_in_init
.Fa "const SSL *s"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_in_before
.Fa "const SSL *s"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_is_init_finished
.Fa "const SSL *s"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_in_connect_init
.Fa "SSL *s"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_in_accept_init
.Fa "SSL *s"
.Fc
.Ft OSSL_HANDSHAKE_STATE
.Fo SSL_get_state
.Fa "const SSL *ssl"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn SSL_in_init
returns 1 if the SSL/TLS state machine is currently processing or
awaiting handshake messages, or 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_in_before
returns 1 if no SSL/TLS handshake has yet been initiated, or 0
otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_is_init_finished
returns 1 if the SSL/TLS connection is in a state where fully protected
application data can be transferred or 0 otherwise.
.Pp
Note that in some circumstances (such as when early data is being
transferred)
.Fn SSL_in_init ,
.Fn SSL_in_before
and
.Fn SSL_is_init_finished
can all return 0.
.Pp
.Fa s
.Sy MUST NOT
be NULL.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_in_connect_init
returns 1 if
.Fa s
is acting as a client and
.Fn SSL_in_init
would return 1, or 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_in_accept_init
returns 1 if
.Fa s
is acting as a server and
.Fn SSL_in_init
would return 1, or 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_in_connect_init
and
.Fn SSL_in_accept_init
are implemented as macros.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_get_state
returns a value indicating the current state of the handshake state
machine.
OSSL_HANDSHAKE_STATE is an enumerated type where each value indicates a
discrete state machine state.
Note that future versions of OpenSSL may define more states so
applications should expect to receive unrecognised state values.
The naming format is made up of a number of elements as follows:
.Pp
.Sy protocol Ns _ST_ Ns Sy role Ns _ Ns Sy message
.Pp
.Sy protocol
is one of TLS or DTLS. DTLS is used where a state is specific to the
DTLS protocol.
Otherwise TLS is used.
.Pp
.Sy role
is one of CR, CW, SR or SW to indicate "client reading", "client
writing", "server reading" or "server writing" respectively.
.Pp
.Sy message
is the name of a handshake message that is being or has been sent, or is
being or has been processed.
.Pp
Additionally there are some special states that do not conform to the
above format.
These are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It TLS_ST_BEFORE
No handshake messages have yet been been sent or received.
.It TLS_ST_OK
Handshake message sending/processing has completed.
.It TLS_ST_EARLY_DATA
Early data is being processed
.It TLS_ST_PENDING_EARLY_DATA_END
Awaiting the end of early data processing
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_in_init ,
.Fn SSL_in_before ,
.Fn SSL_is_init_finished ,
.Fn SSL_in_connect_init
and
.Fn SSL_in_accept_init
return values as indicated above.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_get_state
returns the current handshake state.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssl 7 ,
.Xr SSL_read_early_data 3
